  "title": "Trans-Siberian Railway",
  "summary": "Construction of the Trans-Siberian Railway began in 1891 and linked Moscow with Vladivostok on the Sea of Japan across roughly 5,750 miles. Sources date its completion differently: virtually complete by 1904, with the last section, the Amur River bridge at Khabarovsk, finished in 1916. The line also connected to the Chinese Eastern Railway across Manchuria.",
  "when": {
    "start": {
      "year": 1891
    },
    "end": {
      "year": 1916
    },
    "precision": "year",
    "uncertainty": {
      "note": "Gale (Encyclopedia of Russian History): construction between 1891 and 1916 with the Amur River bridge at Khabarovsk; Oxford World Encyclopedia: major part built 1891-1905; Oxford phrase dictionary: virtually completed by 1904; EBSCO: imperial edict 17 March 1891 and last section 1916. Sources disagree, so the end year is the latest stated."
    },
